From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 02:54:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS-Mythic: footprint reclaim + exec-alloc hardening
 (ml362-ml364)

Three iOS-only changes found by the [phys-map]/[footprint] probes while
chasing the jetsam wall, plus the fix for what turned out to be the real
wall behind it.

ZeroScrub (AllocatorHooks.h): the defensive full memsets guarding against
stale content (LookupCache L2+L1, CallRetStack) committed 48MB of private-
dirty pages per guest thread, ~1.9GB at ~40 threads against a 4096MB jetsam
limit. Verify by read instead -- untouched anonymous pages map the shared
zero page and cost no footprint -- and memset only pages holding stale
bytes. Same zero guarantee. The per-thread stale counts are the probe:
ml362 reported stale=0 on all 42 threads, so the 0x69-quirk hazard these
memsets were written for does not occur at thread construction.

MAX_L1_ENTRIES 1M -> 128K on iOS (LookupCache.h): the memsets were not the
real driver -- a hot thread's L1 hash-scatters across the whole array, so
use alone dirties nearly every page of whatever the ceiling allows (~29MB
per cache measured). Capping the ceiling bounds the fleet at ~80MB instead
of ~640MB. Measured: fex band 1072 -> 733MB, peak footprint 3804 -> 3190MB.
Hit rate was 77% at the 8K minimum, so the ceiling cut should cost little.

CodeBuffer exec-alloc hardening (CPUBackend.cpp): exec allocations come from
a finite JIT pool on iOS, and the ctor's only guard is a LOGMAN_THROW that
is compiled out. When the pool exhausted, VirtualAlloc's result was used
anyway and ClearCache wrote the detection string through a wild pointer
(seen twice, in Arm64JITCore::ClearCache on Chrome's GPU thread). Degrade
instead: halve the request down to 1MB, which freelist gaps often satisfy,
and fault at a recognizable address if even that fails so the honest-fault
pipeline reports it.

